---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Chain from "../components/Chain.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { tokens } from "../lib/tokens";

const counts = {
  primitive: tokens.filter((t) => t.tier === "primitive").length,
  semantic: tokens.filter((t) => t.tier === "semantic").length,
  component: tokens.filter((t) => t.tier === "component").length,
};

const badExample = `/* This fails the build. */
.sk-button {
  --sk-button-bg: var(--palette-blue-600);
}`;

const linterOutput = `✗ 1 problem(s):

  [tier-skip]
    --sk-button-bg: component hook reaches past the semantic
    layer into primitive --palette-blue-600, bypasses mode
    switching, breaks dark mode for this component alone`;
---

<Base
  title="Tres tiers"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="Primitives, semantics and styling hooks: what each tier can reference, and why the direction of the references is what makes it a system."
>
  <h1>Three tiers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A tier is not defined by the folder it lives in, but by <strong>what it is allowed to reference</strong>. Three folders named primitive, semantic and component do not make a three-tier system; the rule about which way references may point does.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header">Tier</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Responde</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">You can reference</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">How many are there</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><span class="sk-badge" data-tone="warning">primitive</span></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">what values exist</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">nothing, is the background</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">{counts.primitive}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><span class="sk-badge" data-tone="accent">semantic</span></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">what does a value mean</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">primitives, and other semantics</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">{counts.semantic}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><span class="sk-badge" data-tone="success">component</span></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">where a value is used</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">semantics only, <strong>never</strong> primitives</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">{counts.component}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The string, live</h2>
  <p>
    Each rung shows the value it currently resolves to. Change the <strong>brand</strong> above and watch what moves: the literal at the bottom changes hue, and every name above it carries on unchanged. That indifference is the whole mechanism.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chain forks wherever a token is mode-aware, because <code>light-dark()</code> holds two values at once. Change the <strong>mode</strong> above and you will see which of the two slots the value is coming from.
  </p>

  <Chain
    token="--sk-button-bg"
    caption="The background of a button, from the styling hook to the literal."
  />

  <Chain
    token="--color-action-accent"
    caption="A semantic token names a purpose and points to a ramp position, never a hue."
  />

  <h2>Why references only go down</h2>
  <p>
    A semantic token may point down to a primitive, or sideways to another semantic token. A styling hook may point to a semantic token. Nothing points upward, and tier 3 <strong>cannot jump to tier 1</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That jump is the one that looks harmless and is not. A styling hook reading <code>--palette-blue-600</code> directly skips the layer where <code>light-dark()</code> lives, so that one component, and only that one, loses its dark mode. Nobody sees the defect until somebody opens the page in the dark.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code={badExample} label="components/button.css" note="tier 3 → tier 1" />
  <CodeBlock code={linterOutput} label="pnpm lint" note="the build crashes" />

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
    <p>
      This is not a documented convention, it is an executable rule. The validator walks the CSS before every build, across <em>every</em> brand, because a rule that only holds in the default palette is not a rule. The output above is real: it comes from pointing a styling hook at a palette ramp on purpose.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The prefix marks the tier</h2>
  <p>
    A primitive reads <code>--palette-blue-600</code>; a semantic one reads <code>--color-action-accent</code>. The different prefix is deliberate: a <code>--palette-*</code> inside a component's CSS is the tell, visible at a glance, that someone skipped a tier. Flattening both into <code>--accent-600</code> would throw that signal away.
  </p>

  <h2>The edge of the tier is a cascade layer</h2>
  <p>
    Precedence between tiers does not depend on <code>@import</code> order, it is held by the cascade. A later layer wins, so brand and contrast overrides always beat the base semantics no matter how the files are imported.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`@layer primitives, semantic, components, overrides;`}
    label="tokens.scss"
    note="the order that decides everything"
  />
  <p>
    It buys one more thing: because <strong>any rule outside a layer beats every rule inside one</strong>, your own app can override a styling hook without a specificity fight.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={`.hero .sk-button { --sk-button-bg: rebeccapurple; }`} label="CSS" />
</Base>
